        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
In the event of an emergency during space flight, it may be necessary for the crew to find extended safety in their Extra-Vehicular Activity (EVA) spacesuits. During this EVA contingency, NASA requires the capability to deliver liquid medications via intramuscular injection to ill or injured crewmembers. The delivery of liquid medication in a damaged vehicle unable to maintain a habitable environment poses unique challenges. Some of these challenges include the behavior of the fluid in a low-pressure and off-nominal-temperature environment, the formation of bubbles in microgravity, and the ability to physically use the medical delivery device at the desired anatomical location while the astronaut is suited. Under the auspices of NASA’s Human Research Program, the In-Suit Injection System project (ISIS) at the Glenn Research Center (GRC) aimed to develop an injection device capable of delivering necessary medications during an EVA contingency. 
<P>
The loss of cabin pressure poses two issues regarding medical injection devices. Due to Boyle’s Law, or the pressure – volume relationship of the Ideal Gas Law, the gas bubble within a syringe will expand as the pressure decreases. Depending on the position of the gas bubble within the syringe, this expansion could expel the liquid medication between the gas bubble and the needle outlet. As the cabin pressure approaches the vapor pressure of the medication, the liquid medication will experience rapid evaporation that will result in freezing a portion of the liquid medication. An adiabatic expansion of the crew cabin atmosphere will result in a temperature decrease. Furthermore, the cabin atmosphere under normal circumstances is used for thermal control of avionics and life support systems. The Intramuscular Injection System should possess the following capabilities: <BR>
<P>
1. The capability to provide an astronaut with an easily accessible, easily administrable injection of necessary medication<br>
2. The capability to inject the medicine through an EVA suit<br>
3. The capability to work in a low-pressure and low-temperature environment<br>
4. The capability to work in a microgravity environment<br>
<P>
The goal of the In-Suit Injection System (ISIS) project was to provide a means for injecting medications into a crewmember during an emergency requiring that the crewmember don his EVA suit. The challenge was to develop a device to deliver medication to the crewmember inside the suit under the harsh conditions of a low-pressure and low-temperature ambient environment. The size of the system needed to be optimized for a gloved hand, particularly in its deployment method. 
<P>
Maintaining the medication at ideal conditions during a period of vacuum conditions is difficult. The ISIS will provide astronauts with a safe and efficient way to administer an intramuscular injection to themselves or others with required medications in the case of an emergency. Each injection will be able to be self-administered in a low-pressure and low-temperature environment.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
A needled injection system was used for this task, much like the Carpuject injector currently used on the International Space Station (ISS). Some modifications were made to existing injection systems, as the surrounding environment is very different. The injector had an outer protective barrier, creating a pressure controlled inner environment in which to contain the pre-measured dose of medication.  At the end of the tube was a re-sealable septum made from a silicone-based material.  At the time of injection, the astronaut placed the septum end of the injector against the septum barrier within the EVA suit. Upon release of the mechanism, the needle was inserted through the layers of the suit and into the body. Shortly after this, the medicine was automatically injected through the needle and into the muscle.  The astronaut was then able to pull the syringe and needle out of the leg and properly dispose of the instruments in a convenient location.
<P>
The suit interface for the needled system described above was very similar to that of the Apollo suits.  A resealable septum was sewn into the Pressure Garment Assembly (PGA) and covered by the suit’s fabric on either side. The needled injection system was pressed against this septum and the needle would penetrate the suit’s patch and into the skin. These injection systems would be able to inject any potential medications that an astronaut might need. Those that could be used would be provided by the appropriate group of medical personnel at a later date. The medications used in the medical injection system would be primarily targeting seizures, behavioral issues, and nausea.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
The ISIS project was put on hold after the preliminary design review was completed. Therefore, no results or data are available.<end>
